# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
ADD("8.1", "math", r"""
<h3>Worked example: a bootstrap by hand with 5 numbers</h3>
<p>The sample has only 5 loneliness total scores: 6, 8, 9, 11, 16. The sample mean is 50/5 = 10. How reliable is this "mean of 10"?</p>
<ol><li><b>Draw 5 values with replacement to form one bootstrap sample.</b> Say you draw 8, 8, 11, 16, 6 (8 is drawn twice, 9 not at all); the mean is 49/5 = 9.8.</li>
<li><b>Draw again:</b> 9, 16, 16, 11, 9; the mean is 61/5 = 12.2.</li>
<li><b>Draw again:</b> 6, 6, 8, 9, 11; the mean is 40/5 = 8.0.</li>
<li><b>Repeat 1000 times</b> to get 1000 bootstrap means. Their standard deviation is the bootstrap standard error of the mean, and their 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles form the 95% percentile interval.</li></ol>
<p>Notice that 16 was drawn twice in the second sample, which pulled the mean up to 12.2. When the sample contains extreme values, the bootstrap distribution becomes wider and lopsided, so the interval shows how much the estimate depends on a few cases. In R this takes one line: <code>replicate(1000, mean(sample(x, replace = TRUE)))</code>.</p>
<p>With only 5 people, bootstrap results are not reliable, because the sample is too unlike the population. The bootstrap assumes that "the sample represents the population reasonably well", and this holds better the larger the sample. With N = 800, the course data largely meet this assumption.</p>
""")
ADD("8.1", "code", WARN("Common mistakes when writing your own bootstrap", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Forgetting <code>replace = TRUE</code>.</b> <code>sample(n, n)</code> samples without replacement by default, which only shuffles the order: every draw gives exactly the same estimate, and the interval has width 0.</li>
<li><b>Resampling x and y separately.</b> For a correlation, resample <i>people</i> (row numbers) and use the same row numbers for both x and y: <code>idx &lt;- sample(n, n, replace = TRUE); cor(x[idx], y[idx])</code>. Resampling them separately breaks the pairs, and the correlation drops to near 0.</li>
<li><b>Not preallocating the vector in a loop.</b> <code>r_boot &lt;- c(r_boot, new_value)</code> works, but it is slow. Write <code>r_boot &lt;- numeric(1000)</code> first and fill it in one element at a time.</li>
<li><b>B too small.</b> For a standard error B = 200 may be enough, but to estimate the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles B should be at least 1000. bootnet's edge-weight bootstrap usually uses 1000 or more as well.</li>
<li><b>No seed.</b> Without a seed, the interval differs slightly every run. Set a seed and report B so that readers can reproduce your results.</li>
<li><b>Treating the bootstrap interval as protection against errors other than sampling.</b> The bootstrap only simulates "a different group of people from the same source". It does nothing about sample bias, measurement error or confounding.</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8.2
ADD("8.2", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: reading the bootstrap results for one edge</h3>
<p>Take the edge lone4 → dep4 (values from this run's <code>R/output/L8-2_cl_edge_boot.csv</code>): sample estimate = {{V:b8_lone4>dep4_sample}}, bootstrap mean = {{V:b8_lone4>dep4_mean}}, 95% interval = [{{V:b8_lone4>dep4_q2.5}}, {{V:b8_lone4>dep4_q97.5}}], proportion shrunk to 0 = {{V:b8_lone4>dep4_prop0}}. One by one:</p>
<ol><li><b>The sample estimate</b> is the value estimated from all 800 people. It is the number you report in the main text of a paper.</li>
<li><b>The bootstrap mean</b> can be larger or smaller than the sample estimate. For medium and strong edges it is often slightly smaller, because LASSO shrinks the edge in every bootstrap sample and sometimes sets it to 0. But this is not a rule: in the course-data results table above, the bootstrap means of lone2 → lone4 and lone4 → lone3 ({{V:b8_lone2>lone4_mean}}, {{V:b8_lone4>lone3_mean}}) are slightly larger than their sample estimates ({{V:b8_lone2>lone4_sample}}, {{V:b8_lone4>lone3_sample}}). For weak edges with a sample estimate near 0, the mean is often further from 0, because the edge is estimated as nonzero in some of the resamples. In short, the bootstrap mean cannot replace the sample estimate; a paper reports the sample estimate and its interval.</li>
<li><b>The lower end of the interval</b>, and whether it is close to 0, tells you whether the edge is "robustly nonzero". A lower end clearly above 0 means that with a different group of people, the edge would most likely still be there; a lower end right at 0 means the edge was removed in a good share of the bootstrap samples.</li>
<li><b>The proportion shrunk to 0</b> says the same thing as the lower end, but more directly. A proportion of 0.30, for example, means "the edge disappeared in about 30% of resamples".</li></ol>
<p>Example write-up: "The bootstrap 95% interval for lone4 → dep4 was [{{V:b8_lone4>dep4_q2.5}}, {{V:b8_lone4>dep4_q97.5}}] (B = 1000)." If the lower end is close to 0, add: "This edge was shrunk to 0 in some resamples and should be interpreted with caution."</p>
<p>A paper should report the seed, B, and whether the bootstrap ran in parallel (parallel runs cannot be reproduced exactly; see the note below the table above).</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes when bootstrapping a network", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Using a global variable inside a custom estimation function.</b> bootnet passes the resampled data to the function's <code>data</code> argument. If the function still reads the outside <code>dat</code>, every bootstrap uses the original data, and every interval has a width close to 0.</li>
<li><b>The function does not return a matrix, or the row and column names change.</b> bootnet needs a p × p matrix with the same row and column names in every bootstrap.</li>
<li><b>Reporting intervals only for strong edges.</b> Intervals for weak edges matter just as much: they often cross 0, which reminds readers that many edges in the network are uncertain.</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8.3
ADD("8.3", "math", r"""
<h3>Worked example: reading the CS-coefficient from a case-dropping table</h3>
<p>Suppose a centrality index gives the case-dropping results below. Each row drops a given proportion of people at random and re-estimates the network; the second column is the proportion of bootstrap samples in which the new centrality vector correlates ≥ 0.7 with the full-sample centrality vector:</p>
""" + TABLE(["Proportion dropped", "Proportion with correlation ≥ 0.7", "≥ 95%?"], [
["0.05", "1.00", "Yes"], ["0.13", "1.00", "Yes"], ["0.21", "0.99", "Yes"], ["0.28", "0.97", "Yes"], ["0.36", "0.96", "Yes"], ["0.44", "0.93", "No"], ["0.52", "0.88", "No"]]) + r"""
<p>The CS-coefficient is the largest proportion you can drop while at least 95% of samples still have a correlation ≥ 0.7. In the table, the largest proportion that meets this is 0.36, so CS = 0.36. By the usual guideline (Epskamp et al., 2018), CS ≥ 0.25 is the minimum for interpreting the ranking, and ≥ 0.5 is good. 0.36 lies in between: you can interpret the ranking with caution.</p>
<p>Note that CS can only take values on the grid (0.05, 0.13, …). The real cut-off may lie somewhere between 0.36 and 0.44, but 0.36 is all we can report. This is also why the CS of in-EI in the course data jumps between the two grid values 0.44 and 0.52.</p>
""")
ADD("8.3", "code", WARN("Common mistakes when reporting CS", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Reporting CS without the correlation threshold (0.7) and the proportion (95%).</b> These are bootnet's defaults, but state them in the methods section.</li>
<li><b>Ranking nodes even though CS is low.</b> When CS &lt; 0.25, even if you plot the centrality ranking, all you can say is "the ranking is unstable and is not interpreted".</li>
<li><b>Confusing the stability of edges with the CS of centrality.</b> Case-dropping assesses how stable the centrality <b>ranking</b> is. It says nothing about which edges are trustworthy; for edges, look at the intervals from Lesson 8.2.</li></ul>"""))

ADD("6.1", "intuition", r"""
<p><b>An analogy for social workers:</b> when you draw an eco-map for a case, you put the client in the middle, draw family, neighbors, workplace and community agencies around them, and use different lines for how strong and how good each relationship is. Psychological networks use the same language: circles are "who" (here, symptoms or feelings), lines are "relationships", and the thickness and color of a line show its strength and sign. The only difference is that the lines of an eco-map come from your judgment in interviews, while the lines of a network are estimated from data.</p>
""")
ADD("7.2", "intuition", r"""
<p>Think of this lesson as "the cv.glmnet of Lesson 3.5, done 9 times": once for each T2 node, each time with the same 9 T1 nodes as predictors. You already know how to do one of them; the rest is a loop and tidying up the results.</p>
""")
ADD("7.4", "intuition", r"""
<p><b>A social work angle:</b> in group work, some members "talk a lot" (they influence others), and others "are influenced a lot" (their mood is easily swayed by others). Out-EI and in-EI separate these two roles in a symptom network. A node can be high on both, or on only one.</p>
""")
ADD("8.1", "intuition", r"""
<h3>An example from social work research</h3>
<p>Suppose you are evaluating a community program to reduce loneliness. The sample is 120 people, and loneliness scores improved by 1.2 points on average from before to after the program. How reliable is this 1.2? If no ready-made formula is at hand (say you care about the <b>median</b> improvement, or the ratio of two improvements), the bootstrap gives you a general method: draw 120 people with replacement from these 120, compute the improvement, repeat 1000 times, and see over what range the 1000 improvements spread. If 95% of them fall between 0.6 and 1.8, you can report to the funder: "The average improvement was about 1.2 points (95% bootstrap interval 0.6–1.8)." These numbers are only for illustration. Keep in mind that this interval answers "how different would the result be with another group of similar participants?", not "was the improvement caused by the program?". The second question needs a control group.</p>
""")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9.1
ADD("9.1", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: labeling every sentence of an abstract</h3>
<p>Below is a <b>made-up</b> abstract of a CLPN paper. For each sentence, decide whether it is a "finding" (directly supported by the data), an "interpretation" (reasonable but resting on assumptions) or an "overreach" (not supported by the data):</p>
<ol><li>"This study followed 612 adult community residents over three waves, 6 months apart." (<b>Description</b>: design information. Check N, the interval and the attrition rate first.)</li>
<li>"Controlling for all T1 symptoms, T1 'Lack of companionship' positively predicted T2 'Depressed mood'." (<b>Finding</b>: provided this edge is really in the table, ideally with a bootstrap interval.)</li>
<li>"The social-deficit side of loneliness may be an early signal of worsening depression." (<b>Interpretation</b>: the "may" is appropriate, but it rests on the assumption that the predictive relationship reflects some within-person process.)</li>
<li>"'Lack of companionship' is the driving symptom of the whole network." (<b>Overreach</b>: "driving" is causal language. Even if its out-EI is the highest, you still have to check whether the CS meets the standard.)</li>
<li>"Community interventions should therefore give priority to residents' need for companionship, which will effectively prevent depression." (<b>Overreach</b>: it jumps straight from observational data to the effect of an intervention. A sound version would be "this suggests testing … in an intervention study".)</li></ol>
<p>After labeling, go back to the body of the paper and check: is the edge in sentence 2 actually in the table? How wide is its interval? What is the CS behind sentence 4? In many papers the problem is not a wrong analysis, but an abstract and discussion that claim more than the results show.</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes when reading CLPN papers", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Reading only the abstract and the network plot.</b> Edge-weight tables, stability results and sensitivity analyses are usually in the supplementary material, and they are exactly what you need to judge whether the conclusions hold.</li>
<li><b>Assuming the authors' λ rule, covariates and choice of nodes were the only reasonable ones.</b> Ask as you read: "With another reasonable choice, would the conclusion still hold?" Did the authors run sensitivity analyses?</li>
<li><b>Comparing edge weights across papers directly.</b> The interval (2 weeks or 1 year), the regularization method and the samples differ, so edge sizes are not comparable; only patterns can be compared.</li>
<li><b>Ignoring who the sample is.</b> Whether findings from a college student sample apply to adult community residents or to an agency's clients needs its own argument.</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9.2
ADD("9.2", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: turning a problem into a review comment</h3>
<p>Spotting a problem is only the first step; it is useful only once it is written as a comment the authors can act on. Take "over-reading centrality" from the ten common problems and write it in three parts, problem — reason — suggestion:</p>
<p><b>Problem:</b> "The discussion calls 'Sleep problems', the node with the highest out-EI, the 'core symptom' and recommends targeting it first." <b>Reason:</b> "Supplementary Table S3 shows a CS-coefficient of 0.21 for out-EI, below the usual 0.25 standard, so the ranking may change after dropping only a few participants; moreover, out-EI reflects a predictive relationship, not the effect of an intervention." <b>Suggestion:</b> "I suggest removing the term 'core symptom' and instead describing the out-EI values and their uncertainty; if the authors wish to discuss implications for intervention, please present them as hypotheses to be tested in intervention studies."</p>
<p>Written this way, the authors know what the problem is, why it is a problem and how to fix it, so the comment is more than criticism. Keep the tone aimed at the paper, never at the authors. The numbers in the example are made up.</p>
""")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9.3
ADD("9.3", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: rewriting a weak methods description so others can check it</h3>
<p><b>Before:</b> "We estimated a cross-lagged network with LASSO and tested its stability with the bootstrap."</p>
<p>What is missing? A reader cannot tell: which package and version; whether the variables were standardized; how λ was chosen (how many folds, <code>lambda.min</code> or <code>lambda.1se</code>, which seed); whether covariates were included and whether they were penalized; which centrality indices were used and whether they include autoregressive edges; which bootstrap, and how many resamples; and what standard was used for CS.</p>
<p><b>After:</b> "The cross-lagged panel network was estimated with nodewise LASSO regression (R 4.5, glmnet 4.1-8). All items were z-standardized first; each T2 node was regressed on the 9 T1 nodes, with λ chosen as <code>lambda.min</code> by 10-fold cross-validation (set.seed(2026)); gender and age were added as unpenalized covariates in a sensitivity analysis. Centrality was computed as out-expected influence and in-expected influence, plus bridge indices with loneliness and depression as communities, all excluding autoregressive edges. Edge-weight accuracy was assessed with 1000 nonparametric bootstrap resamples, and centrality stability with 1000 case-dropping bootstrap resamples; CS-coefficients used a correlation of ≥ 0.7 in ≥ 95% of samples, and rankings were interpreted only when CS ≥ 0.25. Analysis code and simulated data are in the supplementary material."</p>
<p>The rewritten paragraph is several times longer, but every sentence answers a decision a reader would ask about. Go through this lesson's reporting checklist and confirm each item is covered.</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes when writing the methods section", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Writing only "default settings were used".</b> Defaults change between software versions; give the actual values.</li>
<li><b>Not reporting how many people were excluded and why.</b> Attrition and the handling of missing data need one clear statement (Lesson 4.3).</li>
<li><b>Presenting choices made afterwards as if they were planned.</b> If an analysis was added after you saw the results, say honestly that it is "exploratory".</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10.1
ADD("10.1", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: from a vague interest to a question you can analyze</h3>
<p>Many capstone projects start with one sentence: "I want to study loneliness and depression among community residents." Here is how to narrow it step by step into a question a CLPN can answer:</p>
<ol><li><b>Pin down who and where:</b> "adult residents (18 and over) of communities in one city" is more specific than "community residents". Where will the sample come from? Is there existing longitudinal data?</li>
<li><b>Pin down time:</b> a CLPN needs at least two waves, preferably three (one interval for the main analysis, one for replication). How long is the interval? It should match the process you care about: loneliness affecting mood may take months, not days.</li>
<li><b>Pin down the nodes:</b> which items from which scales? The number of items p should not be too large. A CLPN has p × p coefficients: 9 nodes give 81. There is no accepted hard rule like "N must be at least so many times p"; N/p is only a rough quick check. Decide the sample size you need by simulation (Constantin et al., 2026; Lesson 7.6 is a simplified version), then check stability afterwards with the bootstrap and CS.</li>
<li><b>Write it as a testable question:</b> "Among adult community residents, to what extent does each aspect of T1 loneliness uniquely predict depressive symptoms 6 months later, and vice versa?" Note that it says "predict", not "cause", and asks about both directions.</li>
<li><b>Write down in advance "what result would change my mind":</b> for example, "If the loneliness → depression bridge edges do not replicate in T2 → T3, I will report them only as exploratory findings."</li></ol>
<p>This lesson's <code>R/L10-1.R</code> generates a simple analysis plan, <code>R/output/L10-1_analysis_plan.md</code>, covering in order: research question, nodes, estimation method, centrality, stability, sensitivity and missing data. Fill in your own version first, settle each item, and only then start the analysis.</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes when designing a project", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Overlooking ethics and data security.</b> Social work research often involves vulnerable groups. Even with secondary data, confirm what your permission to use it covers, and remove identifying information.</li>
<li><b>Cleaning data without a record.</b> Put every deletion and recoding step in a script and document it in the codebook (<code>L10-1_codebook_stats.csv</code> is an example).</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10.2
ADD("10.2", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: a number-to-file map</h3>
<p>A full analysis produces a dozen or more output files. Before writing the results, make a map of which file each kind of number in the paper comes from; when you change the analysis later, you will know what to update:</p>
""" + TABLE(["Content in the paper", "Source file", "Value in this run (example)"], [
["Descriptive statistics (Table 1)", "L10-2_table1_descriptives.csv", "e.g. skewness of dep1_t1 = 1.05"],
["Autoregressive and cross-lagged edges (Table 2)", "L10-2_table2_edges_AR_CL.csv", "Strongest cross-lagged edge dep3 → dep4 = 0.135"],
["Centrality (Table 3)", "L10-2_table3_centrality.csv", "lone4 out-EI = 0.608"],
["Edge-weight bootstrap (Figure 3)", "L10-2_table4_bootstrap_edges.csv", "e.g. interval for dep3 → dep4 [{{V:b10_dep3>dep4_q2.5}}, {{V:b10_dep3>dep4_q97.5}}]"],
["CS-coefficients (Table 5)", "L10-2_table5_CS.csv", "in-EI {{V:cs10_in}}; out-EI {{V:cs10_out}}"],
["Sensitivity analyses (Table 6)", "L10-2_table6_sensitivity.csv", "<code>lambda.1se</code> vs. main analysis r = 0.665"]]) + r"""
<p>With this map you never copy numbers from memory. In the last column, the first three rows and the last row are the same in every run with a fixed seed. The bootstrap and CS in the course scripts also use a fixed seed and a single core, so they reproduce too; if you switch to parallel processing, they will differ slightly each run, and the output of your final run is the one that counts.</p>
<h3>Results should agree with each other</h3>
<p>Check whether some groups of numbers are consistent: ① in the sensitivity analysis, edge weights with covariates correlate 0.997 with the main analysis, almost no change, in line with Lesson 7.5; ② the <code>lambda.1se</code> network has only 17 cross-lagged edges against 52 in the main analysis, in line with "1se is sparser"; ③ lone4 has the highest out-EI, so the edge table should show several fairly strong edges starting from it (lone4 → dep4, lone4 → lone2, lone4 → lone3). If one result contradicts the others, check the code before you write an explanation.</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes in a full analysis", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Scripts that only run on your own computer.</b> As a last step, run all scripts from the start in a fresh R session (the course's <code>R/run_all.R</code> exists for exactly this).</li>
<li><b>Changing one setting and forgetting to rerun the later steps.</b> For example, changing the λ rule without rerunning the bootstrap leaves figures and tables that no longer match.</li>
<li><b>Treating intermediate results from exploring as final results.</b> Name output files after their scripts, and treat the last complete run as final.</li></ul>"""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10.3
ADD("10.3", "code", r"""
<h3>Worked example: a results paragraph with source notes</h3>
<p>In a finished results paragraph, every number points back to a file. The square brackets below are notes for yourself; delete them before you submit:</p>
<p>"The cross-lagged network contained 52 nonzero cross-lagged edges [L10-2_log; or the 61 rows of table2 minus 9 autoregressive edges]. The strongest cross-lagged edges were T1 Sleep problems → T2 Fatigue (β = 0.135) and T1 Feeling left out → T2 No one understands me (β = 0.127) [table2]. Across constructs, T1 'No one understands me' predicted T2 depressive symptoms most strongly (bridge out-EI = 0.347), including T1 No one understands me → T2 Fatigue at 0.108 [table3, table2]. At the construct level, the loneliness → depression edges summed to 0.557, and the depression → loneliness edges to 0.079 [L7-4_log]. The CS-coefficient was {{V:cs10_in}} for in-EI and {{V:cs10_out}} for out-EI [table5], so …"</p>
<p>How you finish the last sentence depends on whether CS reaches 0.25: if it does, you can describe the ranking; if not, write "the ranking of out-EI was not stable enough and is not interpreted below". Don't leave this sentence out because the conclusion looks better without it.</p>
<h3>Structure of a 10-minute talk</h3>
<p>A talk follows the same order as a paper, but with different proportions: question and significance, 1.5 minutes (why social workers should care how loneliness and depression are related); data and methods, 2 minutes (only N, number of waves, interval, estimation method and stability checks); results, 4 minutes (one network plot, one centrality plot, and one sentence on stability); limitations and implications, 2 minutes (stable traits, causality, how representative the sample is, and cautious implications for practice); 0.5 minutes as a buffer. Give each slide one message and make its title the conclusion, stated only as far as the data support it: for example "Edge weights sum higher in the loneliness → depression direction (0.56 vs 0.08, difference not tested)" rather than "Result 2". Only if you tested this difference with the bootstrap can you write "… significantly larger than the reverse direction".</p>
""" + WARN("Common mistakes in writing and presenting", r"""<ul>
<li><b>Mixing discussion into the results.</b> The results section says only "what was found"; "what it means" belongs in the discussion.</li>
<li><b>Putting the whole 9 × 9 matrix on a slide.</b> The audience can't read it. Show only the plots and the 3–5 most important numbers; keep the full table for questions or the supplementary material.</li>
<li><b>Avoiding the limitations.</b> Raising the limitations around stable traits and causal inference yourself is much better than having the audience raise them.</li></ul>"""))

