# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7.5: the full procedure of Wysocki et al. (2025)
ADD("7.5", "intuition", r"""
<h3 id="full-clpn">The full procedure: the four steps recommended by Wysocki et al. (2025)</h3>
<p>So far, Lessons 7.2–7.4 (and later M8 and M10) have used the simplified "LASSO-only" version that is most common in the applied literature (e.g. Funkhouser et al., 2021): the coefficients LASSO selects, already shrunk, are used directly as edge weights, and expected influence is computed from them. This approach is simple and transparent, and it is what you will meet most often in papers. But it is <b>not</b> the full procedure that Wysocki et al. recommend in their 2025 paper. According to Section 2 of that paper, the full procedure has four steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Combine redundant items.</b> Items with almost the same wording (the paper's example: "I am satisfied with myself" and "I like who I am now") are first summed or averaged into one node. The authors stress that this should be decided from item content and expert judgment, preferably before the analysis, with the reasons reported. They advise against dimension reduction such as factor analysis, which goes against network analysis's aim of focusing on specific symptoms and behaviors; combine only items that are very close in meaning. An automated aid is the unique variable analysis in the EGAnet package, similar in spirit to the goldbricker check in Lesson 9.2.</li>
<li><b>Select edges with LASSO.</b> Exactly as in Lesson 7.2: one LASSO regression per outcome node, with <code>lambda.min</code> chosen by 10-fold cross-validation. The difference is that this step is only used to decide which paths are fixed to 0; the coefficients themselves are not the final answer.</li>
<li><b>Re-estimate without penalty in SEM.</b> Write the selected model as a lavaan path model: paths that LASSO set to 0 are fixed to 0, and the remaining paths are re-estimated without penalty. All residuals within the same wave are allowed to correlate, to absorb contemporaneous effects and common causes within the same period. Missing data are handled with FIML. With more than two waves, first use a rule to make the zero pattern the same across intervals; the authors recommend a conservative rule: fix a path to 0 only if it is estimated as 0 in every interval. Then compare two nested models, paths free across intervals vs. paths equal across intervals (χ² difference test, together with AIC and BIC), to decide the final model. The SEM framework also gives overall fit indices (χ², CFI, TLI, RMSEA). In their worked example, the authors interpret paths with p &lt; .01.</li>
<li><b>Summarize nodes with in-prediction and out-prediction.</b> In-prediction is the proportion of a node's variance at this wave explained by all variables at the previous wave (0–1). Out-prediction is the average, over the variables at the next wave, of the proportion of their variance explained by the node (at the previous wave). Both can be restricted to the cross-lagged part (autoregression removed), the within-construct part or the cross-construct part. They are in units of explained variance, unlike the expected influence of Lesson 7.4, which adds up coefficients.</li></ol>
<p><b>Why the extra Step 3?</b> The paper's simulation study compared "LASSO only" (called ℓ1 GLM in the paper) with "LASSO + SEM re-estimation" (ℓ1 SEM). LASSO alone had near-perfect sensitivity but many false positives: under non-stationary conditions, specificity fell below the 0.80 benchmark in every scenario. The authors also note that the false-positive rate of cross-validated edge selection does not fall as the sample grows, and that LASSO coefficients are biased toward 0. Adding SEM re-estimation and pruning by p-value clearly reduced false positives, giving higher specificity and structural accuracy (MCC) than LASSO alone (the exact values depend on the condition; see the paper's figures). Under stationary conditions, the approaches had similar structural accuracy. The {{V:rec72_min_pop_fp}} false positives of <code>lambda.min</code> against the population target in the Lesson 7.2 table are this problem showing up in the course data.</p>
""" + WARN("Why does this course still use the simplified version as its main line?", "First, it is the most common approach in published CLPN studies, and you need to understand it to read the literature. Second, it is exactly Step 2 of the full procedure (LASSO edge selection). Third, the bootstrap and CS-coefficients of M8 and the main analysis of M10 are built on it; M10 also makes the full procedure a required sensitivity analysis (<code>R/L10-2b.R</code>, see Lesson 10.2). If your paper uses the simplified version, say plainly in the methods that \"no SEM re-estimation was done\" and list the risk of false positives among the limitations (Lesson 10.3); if a reviewer asks, you can switch to the full procedure starting from <code>R/L7-5b.R</code>. The full procedure has its own assumptions too: variables are continuous and roughly normal; and because it is also based on observed scores, it also mixes between-person and within-person effects (see earlier in this lesson)."))

ADD("7.5", "math", r"""
<h3>Formulas for in-prediction and out-prediction</h3>
<p>Using this course's convention, \(B_{kj}\) is the path from k at T1 to j at T2, and \(\mathbf b_{\cdot j}\) is column j of B (all paths into j). For node j:</p>
""" + MATH(r"\displaystyle \mathrm{inPred}_j=\frac{\operatorname{var}\!\left(\mathbf b_{\cdot j}^{\top}\mathbf x_{T1}\right)}{\operatorname{var}(x_{j,T2})}=\frac{\mathbf b_{\cdot j}^{\top}\,\Sigma_{T1}\,\mathbf b_{\cdot j}}{\sigma^2_{j,T2}},\qquad \mathrm{outPred}_j=\frac1p\sum_{k=1}^{p}\frac{B_{jk}^{2}\,\sigma^2_{j,T1}}{\sigma^2_{k,T2}}") + r"""
<p>In-prediction is simply the R² when this wave is predicted from the previous wave only. Out-prediction computes, for each outcome k, "how much of k's variance the single path from j explains", and then averages. <b>Cross-lagged version</b>: in-prediction sets the autoregressive \(B_{jj}\) in \(\mathbf b_{\cdot j}\) to 0; out-prediction averages only over k ≠ j. <b>Cross-construct version</b>: keep only predictors or outcomes from the other construct. Because the items are standardized, all variances are about 1, so out-prediction is roughly the mean of the squared coefficients. For example, B = 0.10 corresponds to just 1% of the variance. This is a reminder that single edges in a CLPN usually have small effects.</p>
""")

ADD("7.5", "code", r"""
<h3>Running the full procedure on the course data: R/L7-5b.R</h3>
<p>The companion script <code>R/L7-5b.R</code> works through the four steps above on the three waves of course data (Step 1: the 9 items have distinct wording, so nothing is combined). The core is a loop that writes the lavaan model:</p>
""" + CODE(r"""
keep <- (B12 != 0) | (B23 != 0); diag(keep) <- TRUE     # conservative rule: fix to 0 only if 0 in both intervals
build <- function(equal = FALSE) {
  lines <- c()
  for (t in 2:3) for (j in seq_len(p)) {                # one regression equation per T2 / T3 item
    preds <- which(keep[, j])
    lab <- if (equal) paste0("b", preds, "_", j, "*") else ""   # same label = equality constraint across time
    lines <- c(lines, paste0(items[j], "_t", t, " ~ ", paste0(lab, items[preds], "_t", t - 1, collapse = " + ")))
  }
  for (t in 2:3) { cmb <- combn(items, 2)                # all residuals within a wave correlate pairwise
    lines <- c(lines, paste0(cmb[1, ], "_t", t, " ~~ ", cmb[2, ], "_t", t)) }
  paste(lines, collapse = "\n")
}
fit_free  <- sem(build(FALSE), data = dat, missing = "ml", fixed.x = FALSE)   # FIML
fit_equal <- sem(build(TRUE),  data = dat, missing = "ml", fixed.x = FALSE)
anova(fit_equal, fit_free)                               # nested chi-square test of equality across time
# if equality is not rejected (p >= .05) and AIC and BIC both favor the constrained model, use it as the final model
fit_final <- if (use_equal) fit_equal else fit_free
fitMeasures(fit_final, c("chisq", "df", "cfi", "tli", "rmsea", "srmr"))
""", "Core of R/L7-5b.R (full script and output at the end of this page)") + OUT(RLOG("L7-5b_log.txt", "Step 2 LASSO", end="Step 4"), "Actual output: R/output/L7-5b_log.txt (Steps 2–3)") + r"""
<p><b>Reading the results, item by item:</b></p>
<ol>
<li><b>First decide the final model.</b> Δχ²(76) = 84.69, p = .232, and both AIC and BIC favor the constrained model: in the course data, the predictive relationships of T1 → T2 and T2 → T3 can be treated as the same. This is a more formal check than the descriptive comparison earlier ("edge weights of the two intervals correlate r = 0.64"). Following the paper, the pruning, the comparison with the reference matrices and the prediction indices below all use this <b>constrained model</b>. The constraint lets each path be estimated from both intervals at once, so standard errors get smaller, as you will see below.</li>
<li><b>Overall fit.</b> The final model has χ²(167) = 249.39, p &lt; .001, but approximate fit is good: CFI = .987, TLI = .975, RMSEA = .025, SRMR = .027. The degrees of freedom come from the paths LASSO fixed to 0, restrictions such as "T1 does not predict T3 directly", and the 76 equality constraints. The simplified version gives no overall test of this kind.</li>
<li><b>False positives (against the population target).</b> The "true cross-lagged edges" in the log follow the definition from Lesson 7.2: the {{V:sem75_n_true}} edges with |β| ≥ 0.05 in the population target (<code>true_B_population.csv</code>), of which {{V:rec72_n_trait}} are small within-construct edges produced by stable traits and are 0 in the within-person truth. Interpreting paths at p &lt; .01, false positives drop from {{V:sem75_lasso_fp}} to {{V:sem75_p01_fp}}, and {{V:sem75_p01_hit}} edges are recovered; at p &lt; .05, {{V:sem75_p05_hit}} are recovered, with {{V:sem75_p05_fp}} false positives. For comparison, with the unconstrained model p &lt; .01 recovers only {{V:sem75_free01_hit}}: the equality constraints add a lot of precision. Even so, {{V:sem75_p01_miss}} target edges are missed. Of these {{V:sem75_n_true}} target edges, {{V:rec72_n_trait}} are trait edges of {{V:rec72_trait_lo}}–{{V:rec72_trait_hi}}, all small, and N = 800 does not give enough power for them. This is a trade-off: the full procedure gives up sensitivity for specificity, preferring to miss weak edges rather than report edges that do not exist in the population.</li>
<li><b>Against the within-person truth, the conclusion changes.</b> The "recovered" counts above treat trait edges as correct answers. Against the within-person truth ({{V:rec72_n_win}} edges), the Step 2 LASSO estimate already recovered all {{V:rec72_min_win_hit}}, but {{V:rec72_min_win_fp}} of its {{V:rec72_min_pop_n_est}} edges are 0 in the within-person truth (the recovery table in Lesson 7.2). SEM re-estimation and pruning by p-value can remove small nonsignificant edges, but not trait edges that "really exist in the population": the larger the sample, the more significant they become. The full procedure, like the simplified version, estimates the population target; to estimate within-person processes you need random-intercept models (earlier in this lesson and Lesson 5.3). <code>R/L7-5b.R</code> currently compares only with the population target. As an exercise, count how many of the edges kept after pruning are trait edges: replace <code>Bt</code> in the script with <code>read.csv("data/true_B_within.csv", row.names = 1)</code> and change the threshold to "nonzero".</li>
<li><b>Shrinkage bias is smaller, but sampling error remains.</b> For lone3 → dep1 the population target is {{V:pop_lone3>dep1}} (within-person truth {{V:win_lone3>dep1}}), the LASSO estimate {{V:sem75_l3d1_lasso}}, and the SEM re-estimate {{V:sem75_l3d1_sem}} (p &lt; .001). Over the paths that were kept, the re-estimated coefficients correlate {{V:sem75_r_sem}} with the population target, higher than the {{V:sem75_r_lasso}} of the LASSO coefficients. Unpenalized re-estimation removes the bias caused by the penalty, but {{V:sem75_l3d1_sem}} is still some distance from {{V:pop_lone3>dep1}}: that is the sampling error of one sample, which SEM cannot remove. The further distance to the within-person truth {{V:win_lone3>dep1}} comes from trait confounding, which re-estimation cannot remove either.</li>
<li><b>In-/out-prediction</b> (full table in the log at the end of this page, or in <code>R/output/L7-5b_in_out_prediction.csv</code>). lone4 has the highest in-prediction (0.318), but mostly from its autoregressive edge. With autoregression removed, dep4 has the highest cross-lagged in-prediction (0.070). The highest cross-construct out-prediction is dep4's (0.005), and no item exceeds 0.005. This does not agree with Lesson 7.4's "lone4 has the highest bridge out-EI": the two indices have different units (explained variance vs. a sum of coefficients), and rankings among such tiny values are unreliable anyway. The values themselves matter more: any single T1 item explains, on average, less than 0.5% of the variance of each item of the other construct at the next wave. When you report to colleagues at your agency, say "most predictive" and "very small effect" in the same breath.</li></ol>
""")
L["7.5"]["summary"].append("Full CLPN (Wysocki et al., 2025) = combine redundant items → LASSO edge selection → unpenalized re-estimation in lavaan (same-wave residual correlations, FIML, test of equality across time, fit indices) → in-/out-prediction. On the course data (constrained model, p < .01), against the population target, false positives fall from {{V:sem75_lasso_fp}} to {{V:sem75_p01_fp}}, with {{V:sem75_p01_hit}} edges recovered and {{V:sem75_p01_miss}} missed (R/L7-5b.R); SEM re-estimation cannot remove trait edges, which remain false positives against the within-person truth.")
L["7.5"]["quiz"].append(("Why do Wysocki et al. (2025) re-estimate the model in SEM after LASSO?", ["Because LASSO cannot handle more than 9 nodes", "Because cross-validated edge selection is sensitive but gives many false positives, and its coefficients are shrunk; SEM re-estimation gives unpenalized estimates, overall fit and a test of equality across time, and paths are then pruned by p-value", "Because SEM can separate between-person and within-person effects", "Because LASSO coefficients have no standard errors, and re-estimation is only a way to get p-values"], 1,
 ["No: LASSO is well suited to many nodes.", "Correct. On the course data, the {{V:sem75_lasso_fp}} false positives of lambda.min against the population target fall to {{V:sem75_p01_fp}} after re-estimation and pruning at p < .01, at the cost of missing {{V:sem75_p01_miss}} weak target edges (R/L7-5b.R).", "No: the re-estimated model is still based on observed scores, so it still mixes between-person and within-person effects.", "The p-values are only a by-product; the main reasons are the false positives and shrinkage bias of LASSO selection, plus the overall fit and test of equality across time that SEM provides."]))
